WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.170
2017 hasn't been Hollywood's best year at
the box office.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:08.320
The rise of high-quality entertainment options
outside of theaters, combined with sequel

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:13.879
fatigue and rising ticket prices, have helped
contribute to a series of domestic flops—leaving

00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:17.800
the film industry struggling to cope with
increasingly disappointing returns on their

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.420
would-be blockbusters.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:24.449
These hugely expensive, high-profile releases
have one thing in common: they all failed

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:26.580
to deliver at the box office.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.119
Transformers: The Last Knight

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:34.010
The Transformers franchise has been a cash
cow for Paramount for years, but the long-running,

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.930
series stumbled with The Last Knight.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:40.399
The movie, which brought Sir Anthony Hopkins
into the fold alongside Mark Wahlberg to expose

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:46.480
the secret history of the Transformers, was
made on a $217 million budget, but only grossed

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.879
slightly over $130 million at the domestic
box office.

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:53.970
There are a few things that can be blamed
for The Last Knight's disappointing domestic

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:58.570
performance, including a slow summer box office
that was unkind to several sequels and long-running

00:00:58.570 --> 00:00:59.810
franchises.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:04.550
The film was also trashed by critics and faced
tough competition from Guardians of the Galaxy

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:08.470
Vol. 2 and Wonder Woman, both of which had
better reviews.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:12.799
While The Last Knight failed to deliver in
the States, it still did well overseas, pulling

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:15.119
in nearly $600 million.

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:19.270
This is still below the worldwide performances
for previous Transformers installments Dark

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:24.400
of the Moon and Age of Extinction, but Paramount
has shown no signs of slowing down with the

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.400
franchise.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:30.420
The next release, an '80s-set spinoff focusing
on the popular Bumblebee character, is due

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:31.780
out in December of 2018.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:33.840
'Bumblebee!

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.720
Stop lubricating the man!'

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.340
Valerian and the City of a Thousand Planets

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:44.850
Luc Besson wowed sci-fi fans with The Fifth
Element and Lucy, but he wasn't able to do

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.170
it again with Valerian and the City of a Thousand
Planets.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:53.100
The movie starred Cara Delevingne and Dane
DeHaan as two intergalactic agents on a quest

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:58.100
to save the universe and was a massive flop
at the box office, pulling in just under $40

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:02.450
million in the US on a nearly $180 million
budget.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.079
Valerian had a few things standing in its
way.

00:02:05.079 --> 00:02:09.770
For one thing, its young stars, while both
up-and-comers, weren't very well known, nor

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.410
was the comic on which it's based.

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:16.210
Reviews were also just average, which wasn't
enough to push through the movie's confusing

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:17.210
advertising.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:22.540
Valerian also faced tough competition, opening
the same weekend as Christopher Nolan's Dunkirk

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:27.460
and just shortly after Spider-Man: Homecoming
and War for the Planet of the Apes.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:29.620
King Arthur: Legend of the Sword

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:35.960
Guy Ritchie tried to launch a franchise with
King Arthur: Legend of the Sword, a $175 million

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:39.590
epic that starred Charlie Hunnam as the title
character.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:45.560
However, the film suffered a massively disappointing
box office run, bringing in just $39 million

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:48.690
domestic to kick off a stagnant summer.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.780
King Arthur was plagued by bad omens from
the start.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:55.790
The movie came out after many, many years
of attempts to make another film about the

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.160
character following 2004's King Arthur.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:01.690
"You've got some heat on ya Arthur, you're
name keeps coming up."

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:06.230
Reports say Ritchie's script was a messy mash-up
that combined elements of all of the failed

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.030
projects that came before.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:12.420
On top of that, the movie saw its release
date delayed multiple times, which is rarely

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:13.630
a good sign.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:20.470
King Arthur also suffered some pretty dismal
reviews, amassing a 29 percent on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:24.800
While the international box office did provide
some relief, the movie is estimated to be

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.660
a $150 million loss for Warner Bros.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.670
"You're giving me a headache."

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.790
The Great Wall

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:36.360
With Matt Damon in the lead and a bunch of
weird monsters on the attack, it seemed reasonable

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.820
to expect that The Great Wall would be a hit.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:44.610
However, a whitewashing controversy and bad
reviews presaged its miserable box office

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:51.590
run, which brought in just $45 million domestic
against a $150 million budget, plus marketing.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:56.040
The Great Wall was set to pull together the
power of Chinese and American audiences to

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:01.580
deliver what should have been a massive blockbuster,
but it proved a domestic dud—perhaps in

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:05.800
part due to the controversy surrounding Damon's
white savior narrative.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:11.590
The movie also received the ire of critics,
hitting just 35 percent on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:17.820
The movie is said to have lost more than $75
million, a monumental loss for the investors.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:18.820
The Mummy

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:23.570
Universal's plans to launch a monster movie
universe hit an early snag when their reboot

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:29.380
of The Mummy made just $80 million domestic
on a $125 million budget.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:33.930
The Mummy started to unravel early on when
it faced dismal reviews from critics, and

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:38.190
a slow summer box office that was unfriendly
towards reboots also didn't help.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:42.480
Many people thought The Mummy was also bogged
down by its efforts to try to launch a cinematic

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.430
universe right away.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:50.090
Unlike the Marvel Cinematic Universe, it immediately
went gung-ho toward setting up something bigger,

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.630
an effort that took precedence over simply
making a good film.

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:55.940
"We're all gonna come up with a new plan."

00:04:55.940 --> 00:05:00.090
While the international box office was enough
to keep Universal from scuttling their Dark

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:06.190
Universe altogether, there are definitely
many lessons to be unwrapped from The Mummy.

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:07.620
Monster Trucks

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:13.720
Paramount knew Monster Trucks, the CGI-heavy
blockbuster about a monster who, well, lives

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:18.560
in a truck, would be a flop long before the
movie hit theaters in January.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:24.000
Viacom, Paramount's parent company, wrote
already off $115 million for the expected

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.000
bad performance of the still-unreleased film.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:33.450
When it finally arrived in theaters, it realized
the studio's worst fears, grossing just $33.3

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:37.780
million domestic against a $125 million budget.

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:46.710
"Kids will love this movie because explosions,
monsters and really fast trucks."

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:47.950
Except they didn't.

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:52.340
At first, the studio had high hopes for Monster
Trucks, which they thought could launch a

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:54.170
Transformers-type franchise.

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:59.520
However, the film was pushed back multiple
times, and it eventually came out to the derision

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:02.840
of critics and was a monster disappointment.

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:04.540
Blade Runner 2049

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:10.280
Sure, Blade Runner 2049 received rapturous
reviews thanks to its stunning visuals and

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:14.580
ability to both update and honor the original
film's classic story.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:19.830
However, its vocal fans were not able to help
the movie make a big splash at the box office,

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:25.450
with the film opening to less than $32 million
at the domestic box office against a $150

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:26.889
million budget.

00:06:26.889 --> 00:06:32.290
One of the biggest issues was the film's extremely
long run time, coming in at a whopping 163

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:37.200
minutes, which may have dissuaded casual moviegoers
and limited the number of showings a theater

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:38.730
could hold in a day.

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:42.550
The film aso relied heavily on fans of the
original film to turn out, and while many

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:48.020
of them did, Blade Runner wasn't exactly a
box office success during its first run, either.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:52.680
In the end, the biggest thing standing in
Blade Runner 2049's way was itself.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:57.169
The film was a stunning visual feast and making
something that beautiful clearly wasn't going

00:06:57.169 --> 00:06:59.450
to come cheap.

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:01.450
Geostorm

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:06.790
The expensive disaster flick Geostorm faced
a chilly reception during its opening weekend,

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:09.780
and it wasn't because of malfunctioning weather
machines.

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:15.230
The movie, starring Gerard Butler as a scientist
tasked with saving the planet after the satellites

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:21.480
keeping the dangerous effects of climate change
at bay malfunction, cost upwards of $120 million

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:28.160
to make but brought in just $13.3 million
in its opening weekend, suggesting an expensive

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.930
failure for the studio.

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:32.180
"It's worse than we thought."

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:36.570
Geostorm was a troubled production from the
start; its release date was pushed back multiple

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:42.139
times as the studio brought on mega-producer
Jerry Bruckheimer to oversee costly reshoots

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:45.550
to fix a reportedly "unwatchable" first cut.

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:50.630
Its fate was sealed when it earned the ire
of critics, who gave it an unlucky 13 percent

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:52.199
on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:07:52.199 --> 00:07:57.120
On top of all of that, the movie hit theaters
at an awful time, premiering just a few short

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:02.830
weeks after a trio of hurricanes devastated
large parts of the United States and the Caribbean.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.710
Talk about a disaster movie.

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:06.720
Thanks for watching!

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.480
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.590
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

